# What changed in OWASP LLM01:2026 (Prompt Injection) — and why

*Author:* Rachel James — Entry Lead, OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications, **LLM01:2026 Prompt Injection**
*Submitted:* 2026-04-29 · *Last reviewed:* 2026-04-29
*Companion artifact:* a per-suggestion [community changelog](#appendix-a--community-suggestions-changelog) lives at the bottom of this post and credits every survey and Slack contribution we received.

---

## tl;dr

The 2026 entry for prompt injection is a structural rewrite, not an incremental edit. Five things changed, in order of how much they reshape practitioner work:

1. **A three-axis threat anatomy** — *(a) how the payload reaches the model, (b) how it propagates, (c) how it is encoded* — replaces the old binary direct/indirect framing as the unifying lens. Every control and scenario in the entry is explicitly mapped against these axes.
2. **The mitigations grow from 7 informal strategies to 11 controls.** Each control now carries a fixed structure: anatomy axes addressed, assumptions, known limits, ≥2 citations from different organizations, and MITRE ATLAS / ATT&CK technique IDs.
3. **Four entirely new controls** — Unicode-class normalization, capability budgeting against the *Rule of Two*, treating agent memory writes as privileged operations, and pinning/signing/verifying MCP servers and tool packages.
4. **Three of the nine attack scenarios are new and incident-anchored** — EchoLeak (CVE-2025-32711), Amazon Q (AWS-2025-015 / -019), and trusted-backend MCP injection (Invariant Labs / General Analysis / postmark-mcp).
5. **A defensive paradigm shift** — adopted from the [UK NCSC's December 2025 position](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/prompt-injection-is-not-sql-injection): there is likely no way to completely *prevent* prompt injection today; controls must focus on *risk and impact reduction*, with no single layer sufficient.

The reference list grew from 14 to 34 sources; the MITRE mapping grew from 3 ATLAS techniques to 9 plus an ATT&CK entry plus OWASP AIVSS. Every "in the wild" claim in the entry has been graded against CTI tradecraft (Admiralty Code) and phrased conservatively — most things called "prompt injection in the wild" in vendor marketing are still researcher proofs-of-concept against production systems, and the 2026 entry says so.

The rest of this post explains *why* each change was made, what research backed it, which community suggestions shaped it, and which suggestions we declined and why.

---

## 1. Why a structural rewrite, not an incremental edit

The 2025 entry was written before three things landed that, taken together, made an incremental update insufficient:

**The NCSC's December 2025 position.** In ["Prompt injection is not SQL injection"](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/prompt-injection-is-not-sql-injection) and the accompanying [news piece](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/news/mistaking-ai-vulnerability-could-lead-to-large-scale-breaches), the UK NCSC stated that prompt injection "may never be totally mitigated in the way SQL injection attacks can be," and recommended defenders shift focus from prevention to risk and impact reduction. The argument is architectural: LLMs make no distinction between "instructions" and "data" — both are tokens on the same stream — so there is no clean equivalent to parameterized queries. (Tier 1 — government CERT.)

**Significant cybersecurity and OWASP community discussion on if Prompt Injection should be called a Vulnerability** Since Prompt Injection is largely considered to be endemic to the way stochastic influence works in LLM, many argue that it should not be referred to as a vulnerability since it cannot be "patched". 

**Nasr, Carlini et al., October 2025 — ["The Attacker Moves Second"](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09023).** Twelve recently-published prompt-injection and jailbreak defenses were re-evaluated under adaptive attack (gradient descent, RL, random search, human exploration, with the attacker given the defense specification). For 10 of the 12, static-attack ASR was near zero while adaptive ASR exceeded 90%. The remaining two were bypassed at ~71% and ~80%. The implication for OWASP: any defense recommended on the basis of static evaluation is likely overstating its effectiveness. (Tier 1 — Carlini's work is among the most-replicated in adversarial ML.)

**The 2025 incident record.** Several production exploits were either disclosed in 2025 or had their first independent corroboration in 2025: [EchoLeak in Microsoft 365 Copilot](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-32711) (CVE-2025-32711, June 2025, zero-click); [the Amazon Q VS Code extension supply-chain incident](https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-015/) (AWS-2025-015, July 2025, ~1M installs reached); [Amazon Q runtime injection](https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-019/) (AWS-2025-019, July 2025); [Invariant Labs' GitHub MCP server vulnerability](https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-github-vulnerability) (May 2025); [General Analysis' Supabase MCP database leak](https://generalanalysis.com/blog/supabase-mcp-blog) (July 2025); [the postmark-mcp npm backdoor](https://www.koi.ai/blog/postmark-mcp-npm-malicious-backdoor-email-theft) (Koi Security, September 2025; corroborated by [BleepingComputer](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/unofficial-postmark-mcp-npm-silently-stole-users-emails/)); and [GitHub Copilot remote code execution](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/github-copilot-remote-code-execution-via-prompt-injection/) (CVE-2025-53773, August 2025). The 2025 entry's scenarios — bare PoCs, pre-MCP, pre-agentic — no longer represented the dominant attack patterns.

**A new normative standard.** [NIST AI 100-2 E2025](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ai/100/2/e2025/final) (March 2025) supersedes the E2023 draft cited in the 2025 entry. The new version names prompt injection at the top level, distinguishes direct from indirect, and aligns terminology with MITRE ATLAS v5.5.0.

The structural rewrite also responded to community input. The 2026 OWASP kick-off survey collected 123 responses; **31 of them mentioned prompt injection** — the most of any topic. Roughly one-third asked us to *expand* LLM01 to cover indirect/RAG/MCP/multimodal vectors, one-third asked us to *reframe* PI as a delivery mechanism rather than a vulnerability, and one-third asked us to *demote, merge, or split* it. Faced with that disagreement, doing nothing was not an option. The third paragraph of [§4](#4-what-changed-in-response-to-community-input) explains how each direction was handled.

A separate Slack thread surfaced four named contributors — **Gavin**, **F Cano**, **Ariel Fogel**, and **Leburu** — whose direct, in-production observations sharpened the indirect-injection discussion materially. Their input is credited inline below and in the changelog.

