## 2. The definition: a three-axis threat anatomy

**The 2025 entry opened with:** *"A Prompt Injection Vulnerability occurs when user prompts alter the LLM's behavior or output in unintended ways."*

That sentence has a problem. It centers "user prompts" — which biases the reader toward the direct case — and offers no mechanical model of *why* the LLM gets confused, which leaves practitioners with nothing to threat-model against.

**The 2026 entry opens with:** *"A prompt-injection vulnerability occurs when input to a large language model — direct user input, retrieved documents, tool output, image/audio/video content, intermediate reasoning, or persistent memory — alters the model's behavior in ways the operator did not intend. Because LLMs make no architectural distinction between 'instructions' and 'data' — both are tokens on the same stream — there is no clean equivalent to parameterized queries."*

The architectural sentence is anchored to NCSC's [December 2025 framing](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/prompt-injection-is-not-sql-injection). It gives the reader a one-line model of why the vulnerability exists and why it is not patchable in the SQL-injection sense.

The three-axis anatomy follows immediately:

- **(a) How the payload reaches the model** — direct user input, retrieved content, tool output, MCP channel, persistent memory, or, indirectly, a fine-tuning interface used to craft the payload.
- **(b) How it propagates** — single-shot, multi-step kill-chain, cross-session through memory or RAG, or self-replicating across agents.
- **(c) How it is encoded** — plain text, base64 or other obfuscation, invisible Unicode, multimodal or steganographic, low-resource language.

The framework is not new in the abstract — every taxonomy paper from 2024 onward picks a subset of these axes. The contribution is that the 2026 entry uses the same axis labels in the controls and scenarios, so a reader threat-modeling a real system can decompose the attack along (a/b/c), check which axes each control addresses, and identify gaps.

A worked example sits in the entry to make the framework concrete. The [August 2024 ASCII-smuggling demonstration against M365 Copilot](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2024/m365-copilot-prompt-injection-tool-invocation-and-data-exfil-using-ascii-smuggling/) by **Johann Rehberger (Embrace The Red)** decomposes as: (a) document/file delivery; (b) single-shot in the targeted session but multi-step in its tool-invocation chain; (c) Tag-block invisible Unicode encoding. Each element of that decomposition points at a different control in the entry — Control 6 for (a), Control 7 for (b), Control 5 for (c) — and a defender can immediately see which ones their system has and which ones it lacks.

The research backing for this section sits in the project's [taxonomy research bibliography](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/taxonomy_research_2024-2026.md) and the [gap analysis](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/output/llm01-2025-gap-analysis.md). The single most useful organizing reference was Brodt, Feldman, Schneier, and Nassi's [*Promptware Kill Chain* (arXiv:2601.09625, January 2026)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.09625), which proposes a seven-stage chain that overlaps significantly with our (b) propagation axis. We did not adopt the kill-chain framing wholesale — it is still a preprint and competes with several other taxonomies that have not converged ([taxonomy methodology audit](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/taxonomy_skeptical_review_2026-04-25.md)) — but it is recommended as a threat-modeling lens under Control 11.

The closing note of the definition section also adds an explicit cross-reference to [LLM02:2025 Sensitive Information Disclosure](https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm02-sensitive-information-disclosure/) and [LLM06:2025 Excessive Agency](https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm06-excessive-agency/), with the reminder that LLM01 concerns the input boundary itself — not what the model leaks (that is LLM02) and not what consequential action the leak/manipulation enables (that is LLM06). That framing is a partial concession to community Theme C, which argued PI is a delivery mechanism rather than a vulnerability per se ([§4](#4-what-changed-in-response-to-community-input) details).

