## 3. Direct vs Indirect: trust-surface stratification

**2025:** A two-paragraph treatment that named the direct/indirect distinction and gave one example each.

**2026:** The same binary is preserved, but the indirect treatment is significantly expanded with three trust-surface strata and a named anti-pattern:

- **Untrusted surfaces** — public web pages, emails from unknown senders, public files. Defenders generally treat these as suspicious. Most prompt-injection research has historically focused here.
- **Semi-trusted surfaces** — issue titles in a public bug tracker, package READMEs and changelogs, third-party API responses, content the user *chose* to retrieve but did not author.
- **Trusted surfaces** — code in a repository the developer owns, rows in the developer's own production database, internal documents, the user's own emails or calendar, content authored by colleagues.

The anti-pattern called out explicitly in the entry: *the attacker submits text into a trusted-by-the-user location through a low-privilege channel (a public form, a customer ticket, a community pull request), and waits for the user's MCP-connected agent or developer assistant to read that text while operating under the user's elevated credentials. The agent — not the attacker — performs the privileged action.*

This change is the single most direct response to community input. Slack contributor **Gavin** raised the pattern explicitly, calling it "backend prompt injection":

> "Backend prompt injections — submitting data into a trusted location (e.g., code repo (github copilot vulnerability), github issue titles, or database entries (Supabase vulnerability). The main goal is to target the backend system by getting a user's instance of the LLM (i.e., developer) or MCP service to attack the backend system (i.e., targeting private repository, install malware in code, dump database, etc.). When it comes to guardrails, these input vectors are usually left unprotected because often times there are legitimate business needs where the content should be unmodified. That makes backend prompt injections a distinct threat vector."
> — Gavin, OWASP community Slack

Slack contributor **Leburu** corroborated the pattern with first-hand observation in production environments and a list of four 2025–2026 incidents that all follow the same shape:

> "In production at Fortune 100 environments, I have seen this exact gap. Content the LLM reads from internal sources gets treated as trusted data when it can also carry instructions. The places I have observed this, internal Confluence pages, GitHub PR titles and issue descriptions, ticketing system descriptions, MCP tool descriptions registered with internal services. The orchestration layer applies less filtering to these because they are authenticated and 'internal' which is the failure mode."
> — Leburu, OWASP community Slack

Several anonymous survey respondents made the same case. Survey respondent (anonymous, **Row 96**) wrote that "as agentic systems mature, indirect injection via retrieved content is becoming the dominant attack surface in real-world deployments." Row 100 emphasized that "agents don't question what they retrieve." Row 41 flagged tool-description injection and persistent-memory poisoning as new vulnerability classes. Row 30 asked us to "expand prompt injection to include indirect injection via retrieval pipelines and third-party data sources." Row 45 asked for indirect to be its *own entry* on grounds of "vastly different risk profile" — which is the strongest version of the argument.

The 2026 entry kept indirect inside LLM01 rather than splitting it into a separate entry, but elevated it to first-class structural visibility: three of the nine common-examples categories, four of the nine attack scenarios, three of the eleven controls, and the trust-surface stratification all sit on the indirect side. Rationale for not splitting is in [§4](#4-what-changed-in-response-to-community-input) below.

The threat-intel position that ties this together — and that anchors the wording of every "in the wild" claim — is in [`sources/threat-intel-incidents-verified-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/threat-intel-incidents-verified-2026-04-25.md). Of twelve incidents verified under Admiralty-Code tradecraft, **zero are tier-(a) runtime prompt-injection campaigns by named threat actors** as of April 2026. Two are tier-(a) supply-chain attacks where prompt-style payloads were the cargo (Amazon Q, postmark-mcp). Nine are tier-(b) researcher proofs-of-concept against production systems. One is a CVE that turned out not to be a prompt injection at all. The 2026 entry phrases each incident accordingly — "researchers demonstrated that…" rather than "threat actors used…" — and where the attack pattern is observed but not the attacker, says so.

### A quick word on jailbreaks vs prompt injection

The 2026 entry calls the boundary out directly in the Direct subsection: prompt injection involves manipulating model responses through specific inputs to alter behavior; jailbreaking is a subset of prompt injection where the attacker goal is to make the model violate its safety protocols. Different defensive surfaces — application-layer for the former, model-layer for the latter — and different mitigations.

This change came from Slack contributor **Ariel Fogel**, whose [Pillar Security terminology piece](https://www.pillar.security/blog/the-terminology-problem-causing-security-teams-real-risks) made the case that blending the two terms is "more often counterproductive" in real defensive work. (Pillar is a vendor — Tier 3 in our citation hierarchy — so we cite it for the definitional framing, not as evidence of efficacy.) The same boundary is drawn by [Simon Willison's canonical 2024 framing](https://simonwillison.net/2024/Mar/5/prompt-injection-jailbreaking/) and by MITRE ATLAS v5.5.0, which makes AML.T0051 (Prompt Injection) and AML.T0054 (Jailbreak) sibling techniques, not nested ones.

