## 4. From 7 strategies to 11 controls — and the rename

**2025:** "Prevention and Mitigation Strategies" — seven brief entries, two to four sentences each, no metadata.

**2026:** "Strategies" — eleven controls, each formatted with a fixed structure:

- *Action verb in the heading* (e.g., "Constrain what the model is permitted to do…").
- *Explanation paragraph* with concrete language a developer can act on.
- *Addresses anatomy axes* — which of (a/b/c) the control covers and partially covers.
- *Assumes* — the preconditions that have to hold for the control to apply.
- *Known limits* — what the control does not defend against, with named adaptive bypasses where they exist.
- *Citations* — at least two, from different organizations.
- *MITRE ATLAS* — technique IDs.

A one-paragraph preamble reframes the section as defense-in-depth: *"Modern guidance and research indicates that there is likely no way to completely prevent prompt injection today, but that controls should focus on a defense-in-depth posture, with each control recommended below considered one layer and none sufficient on its own."* That sentence is the most direct adoption of the [NCSC December 2025 paradigm shift](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/prompt-injection-is-not-sql-injection) and a partial adoption of survey Theme G — three independent respondents (Rows 2, 36, 70) argued PI is a fundamental property of LLM design and that "managed" rather than "eliminated" is the realistic posture.

The internal drafts considered renaming the section to *Risk and Impact Reduction Strategies* (a literal echo of the NCSC framing). The submitted version uses the shorter "Strategies" heading with the framing in the preamble paragraph — a stylistic choice to keep section headings short, not a change in intent.

### What stayed, what got sharper

- **Control 1 (system-prompt role and capability boundary)** — same intent as 2025 #1. Sharpened by acknowledging the boundary is "partial mitigation across (a) and (b)" and citing [Nasr/Carlini](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09023) directly under *Known limits*: an attacker who can observe or infer the system prompt can bypass it.
- **Control 2 (output schema validation)** — same as 2025 #2. The known-limits language is new and important: *schema validation catches format violations, not semantic manipulation; a structurally valid response can still encode an attacker-chosen action.*
- **Control 3 (input/output filters at every modality boundary)** — the 2025 entry had this as text-focused. 2026 expands it to vision encoders, audio transcription, OCR, and low-resource-language degradation, with the [arXiv:2504.11168 finding](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11168v2) that refusal rates can fall from ~79% in English to ~23% in some low-resource languages on identical content. The vision side is anchored on [Clusmann et al., *Nature Communications* 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55631-x), which demonstrated sub-visual prompt injection across all four frontier vision-language models tested in an oncology-imaging study, and on [JPS, ACM MM 2025](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3746027.3754561) for multimodal LLMs more broadly.
- **Control 4 (minimum permissions; credentials in app code)** — same intent as 2025 #4. The 2026 wording makes the architectural claim explicit: provision the *application*, not the *model*, with API tokens. Multi-agent pipelines can re-introduce the full property set at a downstream node, which is named under *Known limits*.
- **Control 6 (structurally separate channel for external content)** — same intent as 2025 #6 ("Segregate and identify external content"). Backed by [Chen et al. *StruQ*, USENIX Security 2025](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity25-chen-sizhe.pdf) on structured-channel separation and by [Microsoft Research's *spotlighting* paper](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/defending-against-indirect-prompt-injection-attacks-with-spotlighting/) (vendor — cited for the capability description; not as efficacy evidence). *Known limits* is sharper than the 2025 wording: an attacker who knows the marking scheme can mimic the marker format; multi-hop retrieval can introduce payloads after initial separation; and StruQ-class defenses were bypassed under adaptive attack in [Nasr/Carlini 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09023).
- **Control 7 (explicit human confirmation)** — same intent as 2025 #5. The wording is sharpened with named failure modes: invisible-character smuggling can cause the *displayed* action to differ from the *executed* action ([Embrace The Red 2024](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2024/m365-copilot-prompt-injection-tool-invocation-and-data-exfil-using-ascii-smuggling/)); approval fatigue degrades reviewer judgment at high volume.

### What is new — and what specific 2025 incident drove each addition

**Control 5 — strip or reject Tag-block, variation-selector, and zero-width Unicode characters at every boundary.** This is split out from Control 3 because the threat model is distinct enough to warrant its own treatment. Anchor incident: the [August 2024 ASCII-smuggling proof-of-concept](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2024/m365-copilot-prompt-injection-tool-invocation-and-data-exfil-using-ascii-smuggling/) demonstrated MFA-code exfiltration from a controlled M365 Copilot demo workspace by hiding instructions in Unicode Tag-block (U+E0000–U+E007F) characters. Subsequent [variant-selector techniques](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/sneaky-bits-and-ascii-smuggler/) reduced cost to roughly two invisible characters per byte. The control specifies the exact Unicode classes to normalize and where (every ingest and every render boundary) — concrete enough to write a regex against. Cross-citation comes from the [Promptfoo ASCII-smuggling red-team plugin documentation](https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/red-team/plugins/ascii-smuggling/), which gives defenders a way to test the control. (Tier 3 vendor; cited for the *capability* — having a red-team scanner for invisible Unicode — not for the product.)

**Control 8 — budget agent capabilities using the Rule of Two as a minimum baseline.** Identify whether each agent simultaneously has access to (A) untrusted input, (B) sensitive systems / private data, and (C) state change / external comms. Any [A,B,C] configuration requires per-action human approval. The framework was proposed by [Meta AI in October 2025](https://ai.meta.com/blog/practical-ai-agent-security/) (vendor — cited as a framework proposal, not as evidence of efficacy) and is independently endorsed in spirit by [NIST AI 100-2 E2025](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ai/100/2/e2025/final) and the [CISA / FBI / NSA / ACSC + allied joint guidance on AI in operational technology](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/2025-12/joint-guidance-principles-for-the-secure-integration-of-artificial-intelligence-in-operational-technology-508c.pdf) (December 2025). Anchor incident: the [Amazon Q July 2025 runtime injection (AWS-2025-019)](https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-019/), where an [A,B] configuration was exploited to wipe a developer's local file system and delete cloud resources after injected instructions bypassed Human-in-the-Loop confirmation. Critically, the 2026 entry quotes the [Noma Security critique](https://noma.security/blog/mcp-servers-agentic-risk-and-the-framework-that-protects-it/) under *Known limits* — the rule says nothing about agent autonomy depth, and a multi-agent pipeline can re-introduce the full property set at a downstream node. Treat the rule as a floor, not a ceiling.

**Control 9 — treat agent memory writes as privileged operations.** Log the prompt and context that triggered each write; classify the content for instruction or role-modification language; require approval before instruction-containing memories persist across sessions. Anchor incident: the [February 2025 PoC against Gemini Advanced](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2025/google-gemini-memory-persistence-prompt-injection/) by Johann Rehberger demonstrated cross-session memory poisoning via delayed tool invocation. MITRE ATLAS classifies this as [AML.T0080.001](https://atlas.mitre.org/) (AI Agent Context Poisoning: Memory). Survey respondent (anonymous, **Row 41**) made an explicit case for elevating memory poisoning, citing [the MINJA paper (NeurIPS 2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03704) at 98.2% injection success and Microsoft's documented commercial-exploitation observations. This control is the structural answer.

**Control 10 — pin, sign, and verify every MCP server and third-party tool package; audit tool descriptions for hidden instructions; monitor for changes in tool composition.** Anchor incidents: [postmark-mcp (Koi Security, September 2025)](https://www.koi.ai/blog/postmark-mcp-npm-malicious-backdoor-email-theft) — a malicious npm package silently BCC'd email content to an attacker for approximately eight days, affecting an estimated 300 organizations (corroborated by [BleepingComputer](https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/unofficial-postmark-mcp-npm-silently-stole-users-emails/)); [Invariant Labs' GitHub MCP server vulnerability (May 2025)](https://invariantlabs.ai/blog/mcp-github-vulnerability) — a poisoned GitHub issue caused an MCP-connected coding assistant to exfiltrate private repository contents; [General Analysis' Supabase MCP database leak (July 2025)](https://generalanalysis.com/blog/supabase-mcp-blog) — a customer support ticket caused Cursor's Supabase MCP server, running with `service_role` privileges that bypass row-level security, to dump the production database into the user-visible support thread. Academic backing comes from [Hou et al., ACM TOSEM 2025](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3796519). The control surfaces *both* an injection path (Invariant, General Analysis) and a software supply-chain compromise (postmark), which is why it cross-maps to MITRE ATT&CK's [T1195 — Supply Chain Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/) in addition to ATLAS techniques.

**Control 11 — test against adaptive attackers; reject vendor or internal claims based only on static-attack evaluations.** Replaces 2025 #7. The case for the change sits in [Nasr, Carlini et al., October 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09023): for most of 12 recently-published defenses, static ASR was near zero while adaptive ASR exceeded 90%. The control names two specific evaluation harnesses to baseline against — [AgentDojo (NeurIPS 2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352) and [JailbreakBench (NeurIPS 2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.01318) — and points to [Microsoft MSRC's LLMail-Inject challenge](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/msrc/blog/2025/03/announcing-the-winners-of-the-adaptive-prompt-injection-challenge-llmail-inject/) as a partially-competitive adaptive evaluation in practice (vendor — cited as a challenge format, not as efficacy claim). The procurement implication is explicit: any defense whose ASR is reported on a static suite should be re-tested adaptively before relying on it.

The mitigations deep-dive that informed this section is at [`output/mitigations-draft-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/output/mitigations-draft-2026-04-25.md). The skeptic-review pressure-testing each headline claim is at [`sources/skeptic-review-p0-claims-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/skeptic-review-p0-claims-2026-04-25.md) — including a correction we made before submission, where an earlier draft conflated two separate Nature-published findings about reasoning models (the conflation has been removed; the Nature paper on reasoning models as autonomous jailbreak agents is not the citation for "extended reasoning weakens the target's own safety," that comes from H-CoT and Mousetrap).

