## 5. Example Attack Scenarios — refresh and anatomy annotation

The 2025 entry had nine prose-only scenarios. The 2026 entry has nine scenarios as well, but four of them are largely or entirely new and every scenario carries an explicit anatomy annotation (a/b/c) plus inline citations.

**Carried forward, with anatomy annotations:**

- *Scenario 1 — Direct Injection.* Same customer-support chatbot prose as 2025. Anatomy: (a) direct user input · (b) single-shot · (c) plain text.
- *Scenario 2 — Indirect Injection via Retrieved Web Content.* Same web-summary-with-image-exfiltration prose. Anchored on [Greshake et al. 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173) and the [NCSC 2025 piece](https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/prompt-injection-is-not-sql-injection).
- *Scenario 3 — Unintentional Injection.* The job-description-PDF and AI-detection scenario, anchored on [Kai Greshake's "Inject My PDF"](https://kai-greshake.de/posts/inject-my-pdf).
- *Scenario 5 — Payload Splitting.* Same scenario; anatomy makes the recombination explicit.

**Updated:**

- *Scenario 4 — RAG Repository Poisoning.* Renamed from "Intentional Model Influence." Now cites [PoisonedRAG (USENIX Security 2025)](https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity25-zou-poisonedrag.pdf) — five injected documents achieve attack-success rates above 95% on standard QA corpora. The cross-session / cross-user propagation is now explicit in the anatomy annotation.
- *Scenario 6 — Multimodal Steganographic Injection.* The 2025 entry described pixel-level adversarial perturbation generally. The 2026 version cites [Clusmann et al., *Nature Communications* 2024](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55631-x) — all four tested vision-language models susceptible — and [JPS (ACM MM 2025)](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3746027.3754561) for multimodal jailbreak via combined visual perturbation and textual steering.

**New, and incident-anchored:**

- *Scenario 7 — Zero-Click Document-Borne Agentic Exfiltration.* Anchored on [EchoLeak (CVE-2025-32711)](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2025-32711) — Aim Security researchers demonstrated that a crafted email could trigger Microsoft 365 Copilot to exfiltrate organizational data without user interaction, bypassing both the deployed prompt-injection classifier and the link-redaction filter. Patched June 2025; published as a paper at [arXiv:2509.10540](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10540) and AAAI Fall Symposium 2025.
- *Scenario 8 — Agentic Destructive Command Execution.* Two events from July 2025 illustrate the same impact through different attack vectors. In one, an attacker compromised access to the Amazon Q VS Code extension repository and committed a system prompt instructing deletion of home directories and AWS resources; the malicious version reached approximately one million installs ([AWS-2025-015](https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-015/)). In the other, a separately demonstrated runtime injection caused Amazon Q to execute arbitrary code through prompt injection alone ([AWS-2025-019](https://aws.amazon.com/security/security-bulletins/AWS-2025-019/)). Same impact, different root cause — supply-chain in one, runtime in the other — which is why the scenario covers both with a paired anatomy annotation. (Per CTI verification, the supply-chain event is tier-(a) in-the-wild; the runtime event is tier-(b) researcher PoC.)
- *Scenario 9 — Trusted-Backend Indirect Injection through MCP.* Three incidents in one scenario: Invariant Labs (May 2025), General Analysis (July 2025), postmark-mcp (September 2025). Slack contributor **Leburu**'s production-pattern observation ("trusted sources carry instructions that the agent reads with full trust") is the operational summary; this scenario is the documentation of it.

**Removed:**

- *Scenario 8 (2025): Adversarial Suffix.* The Zou et al. attack is preserved in the Common Examples list (#5 → #6 in 2026 numbering) and folded into Control 11 as an exemplar of why static evaluation under-reports ASR. As a stand-alone scenario it had been overtaken by more impactful production incidents.
- *Scenario 9 (2025): Multilingual / Obfuscated Attack.* Preserved in the Common Examples list (#8 in 2026 numbering) with sharper figures from [arXiv:2504.11168](https://arxiv.org/html/2504.11168v2). As a stand-alone scenario it duplicated content already covered in Common Examples and Control 3.

The choice to keep nine scenarios and refresh four was deliberate. An entry with twenty-one scenarios is unreadable; an entry with five misses the indirect-injection / agentic / supply-chain reality that the community asked us to surface.

