## 7. What changed in response to community input

This section explains the disposition of every distinct community suggestion. The full row-by-row changelog is the appendix of this post; this section is the narrative summary so a reader does not have to read 37 table rows to get the shape of the response.

The community fell into roughly five non-trivial positions on what to do with LLM01 — a split summarized in the [survey analysis document](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/survey%20and%20suggestions/OWASP_LLM_Survey_2026_Prompt_Injection_Analysis.md) under Themes A through G. Some of the positions are mutually exclusive. The 2026 entry could not satisfy all of them; what follows is the editorial logic for which were adopted.

### Adopted in full

**Theme A — keep LLM01 and expand it to center indirect injection.** Strongest community signal in the survey, with at least nine respondents independently calling for expansion (Rows 30, 41, 45, 75, 92, 96, 100, 102, 109) plus Slack contributors Gavin and Leburu. Implementation: trust-surface stratification under Indirect Injection; three new scenarios (7, 8, 9) that are entirely or substantially indirect / agentic / supply-chain; three new controls (8, 9, 10) covering capability budgeting, memory writes, and MCP supply chain.

**Slack contribution — backend / trusted-source injection (Gavin and Leburu).** Adopted both as a structural change (the trust-surface stratification) and as an explicit anti-pattern in the Indirect Injection prose ("the agent — not the attacker — performs the privileged action"). Leburu's mitigation framing — provenance metadata, author-based trust, capability-based scoping — is reflected in Controls 6, 9, and 10.

**Slack contribution — terminology distinction between PI and jailbreak (Ariel Fogel).** Adopted as a dedicated paragraph in the Direct Injection subsection.

**Memory poisoning as a first-class control (Row 41, Row 4, Embrace The Red Gemini PoC).** Adopted as Control 9 with the MITRE ATLAS T0080.001 mapping.

**MCP supply-chain integrity (Row 4, Row 22, Row 41, Row 100, Row 103, Gavin, Leburu).** Adopted as Control 10 plus Scenario 9.

### Adopted as framing, not structural change

**Theme G — PI is a fundamental design property, not a fully-fixable bug** (Rows 2, 36, 70). Adopted in the preamble to the Strategies section, in the "Known limits" subsections of every control, and in the closing note that adaptive bypass remains plausible. Not adopted as structural demotion (see below) because the community signal for Theme G was about *expectation-setting*, not about removing the entry.

**Theme C — PI is a delivery mechanism / vector, not a vulnerability** (Rows 3-Q9, 5, 64, 70). Partially adopted: the closing note of the definition section now cross-references LLM02 (Sensitive Information Disclosure) and LLM06 (Excessive Agency) explicitly, and explains that LLM01 concerns the input boundary itself. Not adopted as structural demotion because (a) the community signal for Theme A was numerically stronger; (b) NIST AI 100-2 E2025 and MITRE ATLAS v5.5.0 both retain prompt injection as a top-level attack class, and changing OWASP's structure would create cross-reference friction; (c) the 2025–2026 incident record makes prompt injection the most-cited initial vector in agentic breaches, which argues against demotion at this point in the cycle.

### Partially adopted

**Theme B — split direct vs indirect into separate entries** (Rows 30, 45, 96, 97, 100). The structural split was not adopted; the substantive elevation of indirect was. Indirect injection now occupies first-class structural visibility within LLM01 — three of the nine common-examples categories, four of the nine scenarios, three of the eleven controls, the trust-surface stratification, and the most-cited 2025–2026 incident record. Splitting into two top-level entries was declined because it would (a) require renumbering across the entire LLM Top 10, (b) split the threat anatomy in a way that does not match how production incidents actually decompose (most have both a direct and an indirect component), and (c) double the cross-reference cost for readers without adding meaningful clarity. **Row 45's argument that "indirect carries a vastly different risk profile" is correct on the merits**; we addressed it through structural visibility rather than through entry separation.

### Deferred to future cycles

**RAG-specific risks as a separate entry (Row 13).** Indirect injection through RAG is covered under the Indirect Injection trust-surface model and as Scenario 4. A standalone RAG entry is plausible in a future cycle but is not warranted in 2026; the gap analysis flags an LLM02 / LLM05 boundary discussion as the place to take this up.

**Cross-model adversarial transferability in heterogeneous pipelines (Row 102, Q9).** Genuinely novel as a category; the academic literature does not yet have a stable taxonomy. Recommended for future cycles. Mentioned in Control 11 as a testing consideration.

**Inference risk — what can the model deduce by combining permitted information (Row 63).** The framing is sound but the LLM01 entry is not the right home for it. More naturally fits LLM02 (Sensitive Information Disclosure). Forwarded to that entry's lead.

**Provider susceptibility benchmarks (Row 69).** A useful thing to have, and AgentDojo and JailbreakBench are mentioned in Control 11 — but they are evaluation harnesses, not consensus per-model benchmarks. No vendor-neutral cross-model PI benchmark exists yet; deferred.

### Rejected — out of scope (different vulnerability class)

These suggestions raised real concerns but they are not prompt-injection concerns and adopting them into LLM01 would dilute the entry. Per the user's standing direction: "It is entirely defensible to say things were rejected because they ought to be covered in other entries or are not really talking about the prompt injection vulnerability itself but rather impact or risk, or that its some other type or attack or vulnerability entirely."

- **Identity & authentication for AI agents (Row 14, Row 102).** A distinct architectural concern about agent-to-agent authentication. Belongs to LLM06 (Excessive Agency) and to the Agentic AI Security and Governance project, not to LLM01.
- **Context Isolation Failure (Row 19).** An authorization-boundary architectural flaw, not an input-boundary concern. Belongs to LLM02 and LLM06.
- **Lateral Movement across LLM components (Row 29).** A propagation dynamic, not an input vulnerability. The Promptware Kill Chain reference under Control 11's "Adversarial Testing" wording is the partial accommodation.
- **Capability Scope Creep (Row 103, first half).** A governance / agent-design concern. Belongs to LLM06 (Excessive Agency).
- **LLM02 renaming to "Agentic Execution Flaws" (Row 109).** A sister-entry suggestion forwarded to that entry's lead.

### Rejected — naming or structure

- **Rename "prompt injection" → "input manipulation" (Row 47).** Declined. The term *prompt injection* is the named class in [NIST AI 100-2 E2025](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ai/100/2/e2025/final), in [MITRE ATLAS v5.5.0](https://atlas.mitre.org/), and across the entire 2024–2026 academic literature. A rename would create cross-reference friction with normative standards and force every defender's internal documentation to update. The terminology distinction Row 47 cares about (security issue vs known LLM behavior) is real and is addressed in the Definition section's closing note.
- **Merge LLM07 (System Prompt Leakage) into LLM01 (Theme D — Rows 1, 5, 25, 86).** Declined. LLM07 is an *outcome category* — what the model leaks about its own configuration — and LLM01 is an *input-boundary category*. The two are causally related (system-prompt leakage is often the observable artifact of a successful prompt injection) but conceptually distinct, and most security frameworks model cause and effect separately. The 2026 entry adds an explicit cross-reference from LLM01's closing note to LLM07; merging them would force a three-layer hierarchy (direct PI / indirect PI / leaked-system-prompt symptoms) that is harder to teach than the current cause-effect cross-reference.

### Rejected — proposed demotion

**Theme E — demote PI to a vector under LLM06 (Rows 52, 3-Q9).** Declined. Two respondents argued for demotion against nine arguing for expansion; the net signal is for retention with expansion. Beyond the count, three substantive reasons: (a) NIST AI 100-2 E2025 and MITRE ATLAS v5.5.0 both retain prompt injection as a top-level attack class, and OWASP's role is to align with normative frameworks, not lead them; (b) the 2025–2026 incident record places prompt injection at the entry point of nearly every published agentic breach, which argues against making it harder to find in the documentation; (c) Row 3 (one of the demote-leaning respondents) also wrote, in the same survey, that LLM01 must be retained because "core LLM risks don't disappear just because you wrap the model in an agent" — the survey signal is internally mixed even within one respondent.

The full per-suggestion changelog is at the appendix below.

