## 8. Methodology, evidence-tier discipline, and the limits of this rewrite

### How the research was done

Five workstreams informed the rewrite:

1. **Gap analysis** — [`output/llm01-2025-gap-analysis.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/output/llm01-2025-gap-analysis.md). Identifies ten P0/P1 structural gaps between the November 2024 LLM01 entry and the April 2026 research frontier.
2. **Taxonomy review** — [`sources/taxonomy_research_2024-2026.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/taxonomy_research_2024-2026.md) plus two skeptical reviews ([part 1](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/taxonomy_skeptical_review_2026-04-25.md), [part 2](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/taxonomy_skeptical_review_part2_2026-04-26.md)). 21 academic and standards sources audited; OWASP and NIST identified as terminology anchors; the Promptware Kill Chain flagged as sound-with-caveats.
3. **Literature scan** — [`sources/owasp-llm01-update-research-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/owasp-llm01-update-research-2026-04-25.md). Empirical research post-November 2024 organized by attack vector. Each finding tagged Tier 1/2/3.
4. **Skeptic review of headline claims** — [`sources/skeptic-review-p0-claims-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/skeptic-review-p0-claims-2026-04-25.md). Five P0 claims pressure-tested before they could ship in the entry: NCSC paradigm shift, Nasr/Carlini bypass figures, EchoLeak attribution, reasoning-model attack surface, fun-tuning ASR. One conflation found and corrected.
5. **CTI verification** — [`sources/threat-intel-incidents-verified-2026-04-25.md`](https://github.com/cybershujin/Private-Claude-Code/tree/main/sources/threat-intel-incidents-verified-2026-04-25.md). 12 named incidents classified under Admiralty Code; tier-(a) / (b) / (c) discipline applied to every "in the wild" claim in the entry.

### Vendor-neutrality discipline

Per the project's standing rules, vendor sources are labeled `— Vendor` inline in the entry and cited only where (a) a non-vendor source independently corroborates the same finding, or (b) the citation describes a *capability* (e.g., "input/output policy enforcement at the LLM boundary") rather than a *product*. We do not recommend any commercial product by name. The relevant sentence from the project charter: *"Describe the capability, and let readers choose vendors."*

### Evidence-tier discipline

Three tiers, applied to every claim:

- **(a) Observed in the wild** — confirmed threat-actor use, with incident report and CTI cross-check, or government CERT advisory, or victim disclosure, or court filing.
- **(b) Researcher proof-of-concept** — works in a lab, paper, or red-team report, but no documented actor use.
- **(c) Theoretical / speculative** — proposed but not demonstrated end-to-end against a deployed production system.

The blunt summary, as of April 2026: most "prompt injection attacks" in vendor marketing are tier (b). The 2026 entry's Scenario 8 supply-chain branch (Amazon Q AWS-2025-015) and Scenario 9 postmark-mcp segment are tier (a) supply-chain incidents. Most other named scenarios are tier (b) PoCs against production systems, and the entry says "researchers demonstrated" rather than "threat actors used."

### Known limits of this rewrite

Honesty about what the 2026 entry does not solve:

- **The browser-agent attack surface is moving fast.** Brave's [unseeable prompt injections work](https://brave.com/blog/unseeable-prompt-injections/), [Comet research](https://brave.com/blog/comet-prompt-injection/), and the broader trend toward LLM-driven browsing are 2025–2026 developments that may need their own scenario or even their own entry in the next cycle. The 2026 entry covers them under the Indirect Injection / trust-surface framing but does not give them dedicated treatment.
- **Multimodal injection taxonomy is not stable.** The literature has [Clusmann et al.](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55631-x) on vision in oncology, [JPS](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3746027.3754561) on multimodal LLMs broadly, and several preprints on audio and video, but no agreed-upon sub-taxonomy. The 2026 entry treats multimodal as one common-examples bucket and one scenario; this is likely to subdivide in 2027.
- **Reasoning-channel attacks are an emerging surface.** [H-CoT (arXiv:2502.12893)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12893), [A Mousetrap (arXiv:2502.15806)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.15806), and [Chain-of-Thought Hijacking (arXiv:2510.26418)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.26418) are PoCs against reasoning-model safety; the corresponding defense research is just emerging ([Chain-of-Guardrails, October 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21285)). The 2026 entry mentions reasoning-channel content under the LLM02 cross-reference but does not yet have a dedicated control or scenario. This was an explicit editorial choice — the surface is real but the threat model is not yet stable.
- **MITRE ATLAS technique-page URLs are brittle.** Direct URLs at `atlas.mitre.org/techniques/AML.TXXXX` returned intermittent 404s during research; the canonical YAML at [github.com/mitre-atlas/atlas-data](https://github.com/mitre-atlas/atlas-data) is the authoritative source.
- **Some 2025–2026 incidents post-date the submission.** The entry will need a 2026-end refresh.

These limits will be explicitly acknowledged in the next cycle's gap analysis.

